### Key Covered

- **HTML Elements**  
  - An element consists of a **start tag**, **content**, and an **end tag**. For example, the entire structure from `<h1>` to `</h1>` including the text inside is considered an element.  
  - Elements can be **nested** inside one another, such as an `<h1>` tag inside a `<body>` tag, or a `<span>` tag inside an `<h2>` tag.  
  - Elements must be properly **closed** to avoid issues, especially in frameworks like React. Some elements are **self-closing**.

- **Tags**  
  - A tag is the part of an element that identifies it, like `<h1>`.  
  - Tags can have **attributes** that modify their behavior or content.

- **Attributes**  
  - Attributes provide additional information or behavior to tags and elements, e.g., `title`, `href`, `target`.  
  - Some attributes are **compulsory** for specific tags (e.g., `href` for `<a>`), while others are **optional** (`title` attribute).  
  - Attributes can have various values, e.g., `target="_blank"` to open links in new tabs.  
  - There are many attributes per tag; developers should refer to official documentation such as Mozilla Developer Network (MDN) for detailed info.  
  - Common attributes discussed include:  
    - `href` (for hyperlinks in `<a>` tags)  
    - `target` (with values like `_blank`, `_self`, `_parent`, `_top`)  
    - `download`, `ping`, `referrerpolicy` (less commonly used but important attributes)  

- **HTML Case Sensitivity and Formatting**  
  - HTML tags are **not case-sensitive**, but lowercase is preferred for consistency and compatibility.  
  - Proper formatting, such as **word wrapping** and line breaks, improves readability.


### Detailed Examples and Usage

| Element/Tag    | Description                                               | Example/Notes                                                                                                  |
|----------------|-----------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| `<h1>` to `<h6>` | Heading tags, six levels available                       | `<h1>` is the largest heading; `<h6>` is smallest and rarely used unless the document is very complex          |
| `<a>`          | Anchor tag for hyperlinks                                  | Requires `href`; without it, the link is non-functional (e.g., `<a href="https://google.com">Google</a>`)       |
| `target` attribute | Controls link opening behavior                          | `_blank` opens in new tab, `_self` (default) opens in the same tab                                              |
| `<p>`          | Paragraph tag                                             | Used for text blocks; HTML ignores line breaks inside paragraphs unless specific tags are used                  |
| `<br>`         | Line break tag                                            | Inserts a single line break                                                                                     |
| `<hr>`         | Horizontal line tag                                       | Draws a horizontal line across the page                                                                        |
| `<span>`       | Inline container tag                                      | Useful for styling or scripting parts of text within other elements                                             |
| `<pre>`        | Preformatted text tag                                     | Preserves whitespace and line breaks; useful for displaying code or preformatted text                            |


### Important Notes on HTML Text Formatting

- HTML ignores multiple spaces and line breaks inside text unless tags like `<br>` or `<pre>` are used.  
- The `<pre>` tag preserves spaces and line breaks exactly as written, useful for displaying code snippets or formatted text.  
- Some attributes or tags, like those with a **deprecated** status, might not be supported by modern browsers; documentation should be checked for up-to-date standards.


